                "While accounting-based discriminant models like the Altman Z-Score capture historical balance sheet performance, they suffer from inherent backward-looking reporting latency and accounting convention distortions. To capture forward-looking, market-implied credit dynamics, financial economics turned to Structural Models of Default. In 1974, Nobel laureate Robert C. Merton synthesized corporate capital structure theory with the Black-Scholes option pricing model, formulating the iconic Merton Structural Credit Risk Model (frequently known as the Merton Distance-to-Default framework, later industrialized globally by KMV Corporation and Moody's Analytics).",
